        "problem": "Evaluate ${}_{6}{C}_{4}.$",
        "worked_solution": "In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=6$ and $r=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{6}{C}_{4} & =\\frac{6!}{(6-4)!\\,4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{2!\\,4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{2!\\cancel{4!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
